{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Loss Functions Lab\n",
    "\n",
    "Explore the mathematics and behavior of various loss functions: fitting regression lines under different losses (OLS, L1, Huber), investigating the non-solvability of discrete 0/1 misclassification counts, examining classification margin losses (hinge, squared hinge), and exploring the vanishing gradient trap when using squared error on a sigmoid."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import warnings\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import (\n",
    "    LinearRegression,\n",
    "    QuantileRegressor,\n",
    "    HuberRegressor,\n",
    "    SGDClassifier\n",
    ")\n",
    "from sklearn.exceptions import ConvergenceWarning\n",
    "\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
    "np.set_printoptions(precision=4, suppress=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. One Dataset, Three Losses\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's fit regression lines to a clean dataset, and then corrupt exactly one target (first in the center of $x$ as low leverage, then at the edge as high leverage) to see how squared (L2), absolute (L1), and Huber losses respond."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def fit3(X, y):\n",
    "    return (\n",
    "        LinearRegression().fit(X, y),\n",
    "        QuantileRegressor(quantile=0.5, alpha=0, solver=\"highs\").fit(X, y),\n",
    "        HuberRegressor(epsilon=1.35).fit(X, y)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "rng = np.random.RandomState(42)\n",
    "xs = np.linspace(0, 10, 30)\n",
    "X = xs.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
    "y = 2 * xs + 5 + rng.normal(0, 0.5, 30)\n",
    "y_mid, y_edge = y.copy(), y.copy()\n",
    "y_mid[15], y_edge[-1] = 60.0, 60.0\n",
    "\n",
    "clean, mid, edge = fit3(X, y), fit3(X, y_mid), fit3(X, y_edge)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"1. ONE DATASET, THREE LOSSES   (truth: slope 2.0, intercept 5.0)\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'loss':<9}{'clean slope':>12}{'int':>8}   {'centre-out':>11}{'int':>8}   {'edge-out':>9}{'int':>8}\")\n",
    "for i, name in enumerate((\"squared\", \"absolute\", \"Huber\")):\n",
    "    print(f\"   {name:<9}{clean[i].coef_[0]:>12.4f}{clean[i].intercept_:>8.2f}\"\n",
    "          f\"   {mid[i].coef_[0]:>11.4f}{mid[i].intercept_:>8.2f}\"\n",
    "          f\"   {edge[i].coef_[0]:>9.4f}{edge[i].intercept_:>8.2f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Observe how L1 absolute loss depends strictly on the sign of the residual, completely ignoring how big a mistake is. Let's push the high-leverage point further and watch the absolute slope stay perfectly constant!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"   push the high-leverage point further and further away:\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'y[29]':>8}{'absolute slope':>17}{'squared slope':>16}\")\n",
    "for val in (60, 200, 10000):\n",
    "    yt = y.copy()\n",
    "    yt[-1] = val\n",
    "    a = QuantileRegressor(quantile=0.5, alpha=0, solver=\"highs\").fit(X, yt)\n",
    "    s = LinearRegression().fit(X, yt)\n",
    "    print(f\"   {val:>8}{a.coef_[0]:>17.6f}{s.coef_[0]:>16.4f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. What One Residual Costs Under Each Loss\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's compare the literal cost of a single residual across different magnitudes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "r = np.array([0, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10])\n",
    "sq, ab = r ** 2, np.abs(r)\n",
    "hu = np.where(np.abs(r) <= 1, 0.5 * r ** 2, np.abs(r) - 0.5)\n",
    "print(\"2. WHAT ONE RESIDUAL COSTS UNDER EACH LOSS\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'r':>5}{'squared':>10}{'absolute':>10}{'Huber d=1':>11}\")\n",
    "for i in range(len(r)):\n",
    "    print(f\"   {r[i]:>5.1f}{sq[i]:>10.2f}{ab[i]:>10.2f}{hu[i]:>11.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'sum':>5}{sq.sum():>10.2f}{ab.sum():>10.2f}{hu.sum():>11.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"   the single r = 10 is {100 * sq[-1] / sq.sum():.1f}% of the squared total, \"\n",
    "      f\"{100 * ab[-1] / ab.sum():.1f}% of the absolute total\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. The Loss Decides Solvability\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's check why misclassification count (0/1 loss) is non-solvability by gradient descent. We'll survey the gradient over 2000 weight values and try running gradient descent from a bad start (12/60 correct)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "rng2 = np.random.RandomState(99)\n",
    "Xb = rng2.randn(60, 2)\n",
    "yb = (Xb[:, 0] + 0.5 * Xb[:, 1] > 0).astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "def loss01(w):\n",
    "    return np.mean((Xb @ w > 0) != yb)\n",
    "\n",
    "def losslog(w):\n",
    "    z = Xb @ w\n",
    "    return np.mean(np.where(yb == 1, np.log1p(np.exp(-z)), np.log1p(np.exp(z))))\n",
    "\n",
    "def numgrad(fn, w, eps=1e-5):\n",
    "    g = np.zeros_like(w)\n",
    "    for i in range(len(w)):\n",
    "        hi, lo = w.copy(), w.copy()\n",
    "        hi[i] += eps\n",
    "        lo[i] -= eps\n",
    "        g[i] = (fn(hi) - fn(lo)) / (2 * eps)\n",
    "    return g\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"3. SOLVABILITY   (gradient surveyed over 2000 values of w1, w2 held at 0.3)\")\n",
    "for name, fn in ((\"0/1 loss\", loss01), (\"log loss\", losslog)):\n",
    "    gs = np.array([numgrad(fn, np.array([v, 0.3]))[0] for v in np.linspace(-2, 2, 2000)])\n",
    "    print(f\"   {name:<10}{int((gs == 0).sum()):>5} of 2000 gradients are exactly 0.0\"\n",
    "          f\"   max |gradient| = {np.abs(gs).max():.4f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "start = np.array([-0.5, 0.1])\n",
    "w01, wlog = start.copy(), start.copy()\n",
    "for _ in range(200):\n",
    "    w01 -= 0.1 * numgrad(loss01, w01)\n",
    "    wlog -= 0.1 * numgrad(losslog, wlog)\n",
    "acc = lambda w: int(((Xb @ w > 0) == yb).sum())\n",
    "print(f\"   200 gradient descent steps from the same start ({acc(start)}/60 correct):\")\n",
    "print(f\"      minimising 0/1 loss -> {acc(w01)}/60      minimising log loss -> {acc(wlog)}/60\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Classification Margin Losses\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's compare standard classification losses (hinge, squared hinge, squared error on probabilities, 0/1) across various margins $m = y \\cdot f(x)$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"4. LOSS AGAINST THE MARGIN m\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'m':>6}{'hinge':>8}{'sq hinge':>10}{'(sig(m)\u22121)^2':>14}{'0/1':>6}\")\n",
    "for m in (2, 1, 0.5, 0, -1, -2):\n",
    "    h = max(0.0, 1 - m)\n",
    "    print(f\"   {m:>6.1f}{h:>8.2f}{h ** 2:>10.2f}\"\n",
    "          f\"{(1 / (1 + np.exp(-m)) - 1) ** 2:>14.4f}{(1.0 if m <= 0 else 0.0):>6.0f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's fit the same linear classifier to the same 60 rows using five different losses, and check how they perform."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "names = [\"log_loss\", \"hinge\", \"squared_hinge\", \"modified_huber\", \"squared_error\"]\n",
    "print(\"   the same linear classifier trained on the same 60 rows, five losses:\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'loss':<16}{'max_iter=1000':>15}{'max_iter=200000':>17}   stopped on tol?\")\n",
    "for nm in names:\n",
    "    fits, flags = [], []\n",
    "    for iters, tol in ((1000, 1e-3), (200000, 1e-6)):\n",
    "        with warnings.catch_warnings(record=True) as caught:\n",
    "            warnings.simplefilter(\"always\", ConvergenceWarning)\n",
    "            f = SGDClassifier(loss=nm, random_state=42, max_iter=iters, tol=tol).fit(Xb, yb)\n",
    "        fits.append(f\"{(f.predict(Xb) == yb).sum()}/60\")\n",
    "        flags.append(\"no\" if any(issubclass(c.category, ConvergenceWarning)\n",
    "                                 for c in caught) else \"yes\")\n",
    "    print(f\"   {nm:<16}{fits[0]:>15}{fits[1]:>17}   {flags[0]:>3} / {flags[1]}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Why Squared Error Fails on a Sigmoid\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's calculate the gradient with respect to pre-sigmoid logit $z$ for Cross-Entropy and Squared Error, and observe how squared error's gradient collapses (vanishes) completely when the model is confidently wrong ($z = -10$ with $y=1$)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"5. GRADIENT AT THE OUTPUT, y = 1   (cross-entropy vs squared error)\")\n",
    "print(f\"   {'z':>5}{'p = sig(z)':>12}{'CE dL/dz':>13}{'SE dL/dz':>13}{'ratio':>10}\")\n",
    "for z in (-10, -4, -1, 0, 1, 4, 10):\n",
    "    p = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-z))\n",
    "    ce, se = p - 1, 2 * (p - 1) * p * (1 - p)\n",
    "    print(f"   {z:>5}{p:>12.5f}{ce:>13.3e}{se:>13.3e}{abs(ce / se):>9.0f}x")"
   ]
  }
 ],
